1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,059
If you turned eighteen in nineteen fifty-seven,

2
00:00:03,059 --> 00:00:05,793
you were born around nineteen thirty-nine.

3
00:00:05,973 --> 00:00:07,788
You were a baby when the war

4
00:00:07,788 --> 00:00:09,926
began, and a little child when it

5
00:00:09,926 --> 00:00:10,315
ended.

6
00:00:10,495 --> 00:00:12,523
And on a warm June evening in

7
00:00:12,523 --> 00:00:15,811
nineteen fifty-seven, you walked across a stage

8
00:00:15,811 --> 00:00:17,070
in a cap and gown,

9
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:19,938
and somebody handed you a rolled-up piece

10
00:00:19,938 --> 00:00:21,896
of paper tied with a ribbon.

11
00:00:22,245 --> 00:00:23,383
Your mother cried.

12
00:00:23,563 --> 00:00:25,791
Your father shook your hand like you

13
00:00:25,791 --> 00:00:26,472
were a man,

14
00:00:26,472 --> 00:00:28,514
or kissed your cheek and told you

15
00:00:28,514 --> 00:00:29,566
how proud he was.

16
00:00:29,746 --> 00:00:30,716
And that night,

17
00:00:30,716 --> 00:00:31,752
after the party,

18
00:00:31,752 --> 00:00:33,888
you lay awake in the same bedroom

19
00:00:33,888 --> 00:00:35,894
you'd slept in your whole life,

20
00:00:35,894 --> 00:00:37,965
and you wondered what came next.

21
00:00:38,316 --> 00:00:40,871
Here is what most people today don't

22
00:00:40,871 --> 00:00:41,439
realize.

23
00:00:41,619 --> 00:00:44,355
At eighteen, in nineteen fifty-seven,

24
00:00:44,355 --> 00:00:47,314
the country considered you old enough to

25
00:00:47,314 --> 00:00:48,423
work full time,

26
00:00:48,423 --> 00:00:49,903
old enough to marry,

27
00:00:49,903 --> 00:00:52,048
and, if you were a young man,

28
00:00:52,048 --> 00:00:54,414
old enough to be called into the

29
00:00:54,414 --> 00:00:54,784
army.

30
00:00:54,964 --> 00:00:56,453
But in almost every state,

31
00:00:56,453 --> 00:00:58,285
you were not old enough to vote.

32
00:00:58,465 --> 00:01:01,321
That wouldn't change for another fourteen years.

33
00:01:01,671 --> 00:01:04,598
So what did adulthood really look like,

34
00:01:04,598 --> 00:01:08,277
for an eighteen-year-old in nineteen fifty-seven?

35
00:01:08,457 --> 00:01:10,268
The jobs, the paychecks,

36
00:01:10,268 --> 00:01:11,702
the rent, the cars,

37
00:01:11,702 --> 00:01:13,739
the dates and the weddings.

38
00:01:13,919 --> 00:01:14,824
And the strange,

39
00:01:14,824 --> 00:01:17,539
frightening autumn when the whole country looked

40
00:01:17,539 --> 00:01:18,331
up at the sky.

41
00:01:18,511 --> 00:01:20,310
Let's go back and find out.

42
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:23,917
High school graduation in nineteen fifty-seven meant

43
00:01:23,917 --> 00:01:25,421
more than it does today.

44
00:01:25,601 --> 00:01:27,022
For a lot of families,

45
00:01:27,022 --> 00:01:29,153
you were the first one to finish.

46
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:31,174
Your parents had grown up in the

47
00:01:31,174 --> 00:01:31,806
Depression.

48
00:01:31,986 --> 00:01:34,411
Many of them had left school at

49
00:01:34,411 --> 00:01:37,227
fourteen or fifteen to help put food

50
00:01:37,227 --> 00:01:38,244
on the table.

51
00:01:38,424 --> 00:01:39,539
So that diploma,

52
00:01:39,539 --> 00:01:41,350
in its stiff little cover,

53
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:43,788
went straight onto the mantelpiece.

54
00:01:44,138 --> 00:01:45,695
There was the yearbook,

55
00:01:45,695 --> 00:01:46,982
full of signatures.

56
00:01:47,162 --> 00:01:48,195
Never change.

57
00:01:48,375 --> 00:01:49,385
Stay sweet.

58
00:01:49,565 --> 00:01:51,028
Good luck in everything.

59
00:01:51,208 --> 00:01:53,070
There was the class ring you'd paid

60
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:54,134
for in installments.

61
00:01:54,314 --> 00:01:56,415
And there was the senior prom a

62
00:01:56,415 --> 00:01:57,567
few weeks before,

63
00:01:57,567 --> 00:02:00,075
in a gymnasium hung with crepe paper,

64
00:02:00,075 --> 00:02:02,718
the girls in full-skirted gowns and the

65
00:02:02,718 --> 00:02:05,158
boys in rented white dinner jackets.

66
00:02:05,508 --> 00:02:08,072
The senior class picture was taken on

67
00:02:08,072 --> 00:02:10,151
the front steps of the school,

68
00:02:10,151 --> 00:02:12,438
everybody squinting into the sun,

69
00:02:12,438 --> 00:02:14,517
the tall boys in the back row.

70
00:02:14,697 --> 00:02:16,353
Somewhere in a drawer,

71
00:02:16,353 --> 00:02:18,234
or in a box in the attic,

72
00:02:18,234 --> 00:02:21,245
you probably still have that photograph.

73
00:02:21,425 --> 00:02:23,063
And if you look closely,

74
00:02:23,063 --> 00:02:25,520
you can still name almost every face

75
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:25,930
in it.

76
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:27,707
After the ceremony,

77
00:02:27,707 --> 00:02:29,886
some of the kids drove out to

78
00:02:29,886 --> 00:02:32,291
the lake, or to somebody's farm,

79
00:02:32,291 --> 00:02:34,620
and sat around a fire until the

80
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:35,672
sky got light.

81
00:02:35,852 --> 00:02:38,116
You made promises to stay in touch.

82
00:02:38,296 --> 00:02:39,364
Some of you did.

83
00:02:39,544 --> 00:02:40,752
Most of you didn't.

84
00:02:40,932 --> 00:02:41,867
Within a year,

85
00:02:41,867 --> 00:02:44,674
that whole class would be scattered across

86
00:02:44,674 --> 00:02:46,344
the country, in the army,

87
00:02:46,344 --> 00:02:48,015
in offices, in factories,

88
00:02:48,015 --> 00:02:49,685
in kitchens of their own.

89
00:02:50,036 --> 00:02:52,199
At the commencement ceremony,

90
00:02:52,199 --> 00:02:55,183
the speaker, usually a local minister or

91
00:02:55,183 --> 00:02:57,794
the mayor or a proud old principal,

92
00:02:57,794 --> 00:03:00,480
told you that you were stepping into

93
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:01,599
the atomic age.

94
00:03:01,779 --> 00:03:03,621
A world of jet planes,

95
00:03:03,621 --> 00:03:07,723
television, and wonders nobody could imagine yet.

96
00:03:07,903 --> 00:03:09,749
He said the future belonged to you.

97
00:03:09,929 --> 00:03:10,770
And for once,

98
00:03:10,770 --> 00:03:13,099
the grown-ups were not exaggerating.

99
00:03:13,449 --> 00:03:14,290
For the girls,

100
00:03:14,290 --> 00:03:16,031
the questions were different,

101
00:03:16,031 --> 00:03:18,314
and often the answers had already been

102
00:03:18,314 --> 00:03:19,335
decided for them.

103
00:03:19,515 --> 00:03:21,558
A job, for a little while.

104
00:03:21,738 --> 00:03:23,782
Maybe secretarial school.

105
00:03:23,962 --> 00:03:25,041
Maybe nursing.

106
00:03:25,221 --> 00:03:27,755
And then, everyone assumed,

107
00:03:27,755 --> 00:03:28,600
marriage.

108
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:29,589
For the boys,

109
00:03:29,589 --> 00:03:31,893
there was one question that hung over

110
00:03:31,893 --> 00:03:32,890
everything else.

111
00:03:33,070 --> 00:03:34,161
What about the draft?

112
00:03:34,511 --> 00:03:36,358
In nineteen fifty-seven,

113
00:03:36,358 --> 00:03:38,667
America had a peacetime draft.

114
00:03:38,847 --> 00:03:41,029
Every young man had to register with

115
00:03:41,029 --> 00:03:43,816
the Selective Service when he turned eighteen.

116
00:03:43,996 --> 00:03:46,485
You walked into the local board office,

117
00:03:46,485 --> 00:03:47,634
filled out a card,

118
00:03:47,634 --> 00:03:49,612
and they gave you a small paper

119
00:03:49,612 --> 00:03:50,697
card of your own,

120
00:03:50,697 --> 00:03:53,058
which you were supposed to carry with

121
00:03:53,058 --> 00:03:54,143
you at all times.

122
00:03:54,493 --> 00:03:56,211
You might not be called right away.

123
00:03:56,391 --> 00:03:59,352
Some young men were deferred for college,

124
00:03:59,352 --> 00:04:00,580
or for farm work,

125
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:02,891
or for a family that depended on

126
00:04:02,891 --> 00:04:03,252
them.

127
00:04:03,432 --> 00:04:06,430
But a lot of eighteen and nineteen-year-olds

128
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:07,998
simply chose to enlist,

129
00:04:07,998 --> 00:04:09,360
to get it over with,

130
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:12,018
or to pick their branch before somebody

131
00:04:12,018 --> 00:04:13,312
picked it for them.

132
00:04:13,492 --> 00:04:14,131
The Navy.

133
00:04:14,311 --> 00:04:15,402
The Air Force.

134
00:04:15,582 --> 00:04:16,558
The Marines.

135
00:04:16,908 --> 00:04:18,559
There was another option,

136
00:04:18,559 --> 00:04:18,823
too.

137
00:04:19,003 --> 00:04:21,846
Under a law passed in nineteen fifty-five,

138
00:04:21,846 --> 00:04:24,282
a young man could join the reserves,

139
00:04:24,282 --> 00:04:26,921
serve around six months of active duty,

140
00:04:26,921 --> 00:04:29,425
and then spend the next several years

141
00:04:29,425 --> 00:04:32,064
training on weekends and in the summer.

142
00:04:32,244 --> 00:04:34,331
For a boy who wanted to start

143
00:04:34,331 --> 00:04:36,489
a job or marry his sweetheart,

144
00:04:36,489 --> 00:04:39,079
that was often the choice his father

145
00:04:39,079 --> 00:04:39,942
recommended.

146
00:04:40,292 --> 00:04:43,167
Two years of active duty was typical

147
00:04:43,167 --> 00:04:44,285
for a draftee.

148
00:04:44,465 --> 00:04:45,550
You got on a bus,

149
00:04:45,550 --> 00:04:46,634
with a small bag,

150
00:04:46,634 --> 00:04:48,993
and your mother standing on the curb.

151
00:04:49,173 --> 00:04:51,130
And a few weeks later you had

152
00:04:51,130 --> 00:04:52,075
a shaved head,

153
00:04:52,075 --> 00:04:53,155
a pair of boots,

154
00:04:53,155 --> 00:04:55,449
and a drill sergeant who seemed to

155
00:04:55,449 --> 00:04:57,945
know your name before you'd ever said

156
00:04:57,945 --> 00:04:58,148
it.

157
00:04:58,498 --> 00:05:00,030
And then there were the letters.

158
00:05:00,210 --> 00:05:01,945
Every evening at mail call,

159
00:05:01,945 --> 00:05:04,001
the sergeant read out the names,

160
00:05:04,001 --> 00:05:05,864
and you waited to hear yours.

161
00:05:06,044 --> 00:05:07,591
A letter from your mother,

162
00:05:07,591 --> 00:05:09,376
with news about the neighbors.

163
00:05:09,556 --> 00:05:11,302
A letter from your girl,

164
00:05:11,302 --> 00:05:14,429
sometimes perfumed, which you read three or

165
00:05:14,429 --> 00:05:17,265
four times and kept in your footlocker.

166
00:05:17,445 --> 00:05:18,516
In those years,

167
00:05:18,516 --> 00:05:20,801
a letter was the only line home.

168
00:05:21,151 --> 00:05:23,310
Even the most famous young man in

169
00:05:23,310 --> 00:05:24,750
America wasn't spared.

170
00:05:24,930 --> 00:05:27,729
In December of nineteen fifty-seven,

171
00:05:27,729 --> 00:05:30,839
Elvis Presley received his draft notice.

172
00:05:31,019 --> 00:05:33,470
He was inducted the following March,

173
00:05:33,470 --> 00:05:36,671
and the newspapers photographed the army barber

174
00:05:36,671 --> 00:05:38,577
cutting off his famous hair.

175
00:05:38,757 --> 00:05:40,708
If the king of rock and roll

176
00:05:40,708 --> 00:05:41,404
had to go,

177
00:05:41,404 --> 00:05:44,051
everybody understood that you did too.

178
00:05:44,402 --> 00:05:46,853
For many young men from small towns,

179
00:05:46,853 --> 00:05:49,237
the service was the first time they

180
00:05:49,237 --> 00:05:51,008
had ever left their state.

181
00:05:51,188 --> 00:05:52,704
They saw Germany,

182
00:05:52,704 --> 00:05:54,398
or Korea, or Texas,

183
00:05:54,398 --> 00:05:57,074
which seemed just as far away.

184
00:05:57,254 --> 00:05:59,228
They learned to fix engines,

185
00:05:59,228 --> 00:06:01,626
run radios, and get along with men

186
00:06:01,626 --> 00:06:03,953
from every corner of the country.

187
00:06:04,133 --> 00:06:05,951
And a lot of them came home

188
00:06:05,951 --> 00:06:06,490
changed.

189
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:08,613
Some of the young men of the

190
00:06:08,613 --> 00:06:11,400
class of nineteen fifty-seven would make the

191
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:12,540
army their career.

192
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:15,663
Others counted down every single day until

193
00:06:15,663 --> 00:06:16,784
their discharge.

194
00:06:16,964 --> 00:06:18,631
But almost all of them,

195
00:06:18,631 --> 00:06:21,387
years later, would say the same thing.

196
00:06:21,567 --> 00:06:23,919
The service taught them how to work,

197
00:06:23,919 --> 00:06:25,290
how to follow orders,

198
00:06:25,290 --> 00:06:27,185
and how to look after the man

199
00:06:27,185 --> 00:06:28,034
next to them.

200
00:06:28,384 --> 00:06:29,935
If you weren't in uniform,

201
00:06:29,935 --> 00:06:30,949
you went to work.

202
00:06:31,129 --> 00:06:33,087
And in nineteen fifty-seven,

203
00:06:33,087 --> 00:06:35,395
a young person with a high school

204
00:06:35,395 --> 00:06:37,143
diploma could find a job.

205
00:06:37,323 --> 00:06:39,111
The factories were busy.

206
00:06:39,291 --> 00:06:41,697
The new suburbs needed builders,

207
00:06:41,697 --> 00:06:43,726
plumbers, and electricians.

208
00:06:43,906 --> 00:06:47,517
The offices downtown needed clerks and typists.

209
00:06:47,697 --> 00:06:50,298
The phone company needed operators.

210
00:06:50,648 --> 00:06:53,293
Maybe you walked into the personnel office

211
00:06:53,293 --> 00:06:55,434
at the plant in your best clothes,

212
00:06:55,434 --> 00:06:56,820
and filled out a form,

213
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:58,206
and started on Monday.

214
00:06:58,386 --> 00:06:59,149
On the line,

215
00:06:59,149 --> 00:07:01,057
the work was hard and loud and

216
00:07:01,057 --> 00:07:03,601
repetitive, but the union had fought for

217
00:07:03,601 --> 00:07:05,573
good wages, and the plant had a

218
00:07:05,573 --> 00:07:08,245
pension, and your father might have worked

219
00:07:08,245 --> 00:07:10,216
there for twenty years already.

220
00:07:10,567 --> 00:07:12,638
You punched a time clock at seven

221
00:07:12,638 --> 00:07:13,580
in the morning,

222
00:07:13,580 --> 00:07:16,028
carrying a metal lunch pail your mother

223
00:07:16,028 --> 00:07:17,535
packed the night before,

224
00:07:17,535 --> 00:07:19,669
the same kind your father carried.

225
00:07:19,849 --> 00:07:21,576
At noon a whistle blew,

226
00:07:21,576 --> 00:07:24,505
and everybody sat on crates and benches

227
00:07:24,505 --> 00:07:25,782
and ate together.

228
00:07:25,962 --> 00:07:28,679
At three-thirty, the whistle blew again,

229
00:07:28,679 --> 00:07:31,057
and the parking lot emptied in five

230
00:07:31,057 --> 00:07:31,941
minutes flat.

231
00:07:32,291 --> 00:07:34,164
If you were a young woman,

232
00:07:34,164 --> 00:07:36,325
the job might be in an office,

233
00:07:36,325 --> 00:07:38,343
in a long room full of desks

234
00:07:38,343 --> 00:07:40,000
called the typing pool.

235
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:42,302
You sat in rows with the other

236
00:07:42,302 --> 00:07:45,839
girls, typing letters on heavy manual typewriters,

237
00:07:45,839 --> 00:07:48,457
with carbon paper between the sheets,

238
00:07:48,457 --> 00:07:50,791
under the eye of a supervisor who

239
00:07:50,791 --> 00:07:53,055
checked every page for mistakes.

240
00:07:53,405 --> 00:07:55,032
Before you got that job,

241
00:07:55,032 --> 00:07:57,812
you might have taken typing and shorthand

242
00:07:57,812 --> 00:07:58,830
in high school,

243
00:07:58,830 --> 00:08:00,593
or spent a few months at a

244
00:08:00,593 --> 00:08:02,356
business college downtown.

245
00:08:02,536 --> 00:08:04,764
A girl who could type sixty words

246
00:08:04,764 --> 00:08:07,329
a minute, take dictation in shorthand,

247
00:08:07,329 --> 00:08:09,894
and answer the telephone in a pleasant

248
00:08:09,894 --> 00:08:11,986
voice was in demand everywhere.

249
00:08:12,166 --> 00:08:14,186
Those skills paid the rent for a

250
00:08:14,186 --> 00:08:15,638
great many young women.

251
00:08:15,988 --> 00:08:18,638
Or you might be a telephone operator,

252
00:08:18,638 --> 00:08:19,927
wearing a headset,

253
00:08:19,927 --> 00:08:22,219
sitting in front of a great wall

254
00:08:22,219 --> 00:08:23,580
of plugs and cords,

255
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:25,728
connecting calls all day long.

256
00:08:25,908 --> 00:08:26,953
Number, please.

257
00:08:27,133 --> 00:08:29,049
You said it a thousand times a

258
00:08:29,049 --> 00:08:29,432
shift.

259
00:08:29,612 --> 00:08:31,820
Or you worked behind the counter at

260
00:08:31,820 --> 00:08:33,712
the department store downtown,

261
00:08:33,712 --> 00:08:34,721
in a neat dress,

262
00:08:34,721 --> 00:08:36,172
with your hair just so.

263
00:08:36,522 --> 00:08:38,367
The boys who didn't go to the

264
00:08:38,367 --> 00:08:39,830
plant found other work.

265
00:08:40,010 --> 00:08:42,520
Pumping gas and checking oil at the

266
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:43,668
filling station.

267
00:08:43,848 --> 00:08:46,611
Bagging groceries at the new supermarket.

268
00:08:46,791 --> 00:08:48,939
Loading trucks at the lumber yard.

269
00:08:49,119 --> 00:08:51,566
Working behind the soda fountain at the

270
00:08:51,566 --> 00:08:53,574
drugstore, in a white paper hat,

271
00:08:53,574 --> 00:08:55,708
making malts for the kids who were

272
00:08:55,708 --> 00:08:56,712
still in school.

273
00:08:57,062 --> 00:08:59,913
The federal minimum wage was one dollar

274
00:08:59,913 --> 00:09:00,498
an hour.

275
00:09:00,678 --> 00:09:03,931
According to the Social Security Administration,

276
00:09:03,931 --> 00:09:06,845
the average American worker earned a little

277
00:09:06,845 --> 00:09:08,200
over three thousand,

278
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:10,233
six hundred dollars that year.

279
00:09:10,413 --> 00:09:12,963
For an eighteen-year-old starting out,

280
00:09:12,963 --> 00:09:13,768
it was less.

281
00:09:13,948 --> 00:09:15,870
But you'd never had that much money

282
00:09:15,870 --> 00:09:16,584
in your life.

283
00:09:16,934 --> 00:09:19,477
Your first paycheck came on a Friday,

284
00:09:19,477 --> 00:09:22,226
sometimes in a small brown envelope with

285
00:09:22,226 --> 00:09:24,701
the cash already counted out inside.

286
00:09:24,881 --> 00:09:26,571
And in a lot of families,

287
00:09:26,571 --> 00:09:27,923
the rule was simple.

288
00:09:28,103 --> 00:09:29,368
If you lived at home,

289
00:09:29,368 --> 00:09:30,994
you gave some of it to your

290
00:09:30,994 --> 00:09:32,561
mother for room and board.

291
00:09:32,741 --> 00:09:34,099
The rest was yours.

292
00:09:34,279 --> 00:09:36,254
You'd never felt so grown up in

293
00:09:36,254 --> 00:09:36,891
your life.

294
00:09:37,242 --> 00:09:38,148
And on a farm,

295
00:09:38,148 --> 00:09:39,897
the job was the same one it

296
00:09:39,897 --> 00:09:40,934
had always been.

297
00:09:41,114 --> 00:09:42,468
Up before dawn,

298
00:09:42,468 --> 00:09:45,357
milking, feeding, fixing fences,

299
00:09:45,357 --> 00:09:47,975
and the long days of harvest.

300
00:09:48,155 --> 00:09:49,725
Many farm kids stayed,

301
00:09:49,725 --> 00:09:52,079
and took over from their fathers.

302
00:09:52,259 --> 00:09:54,733
Many others left for the cities,

303
00:09:54,733 --> 00:09:58,213
because a factory paycheck came every Friday,

304
00:09:58,213 --> 00:09:59,295
rain or shine.

305
00:09:59,645 --> 00:10:02,063
You also got your Social Security card,

306
00:10:02,063 --> 00:10:03,986
if you didn't have one already,

307
00:10:03,986 --> 00:10:06,094
and you watched them take a little

308
00:10:06,094 --> 00:10:07,272
out of every check.

309
00:10:07,452 --> 00:10:09,067
You didn't think much about it at

310
00:10:09,067 --> 00:10:09,507
the time.

311
00:10:09,687 --> 00:10:11,327
Nearly seventy years later,

312
00:10:11,327 --> 00:10:12,967
a lot of you are still glad

313
00:10:12,967 --> 00:10:13,635
you didn't.

314
00:10:13,985 --> 00:10:18,422
Most eighteen-year-olds in nineteen fifty-seven still lived

315
00:10:18,422 --> 00:10:19,023
at home.

316
00:10:19,203 --> 00:10:21,593
Moving out usually meant one of two

317
00:10:21,593 --> 00:10:22,071
things.

318
00:10:22,251 --> 00:10:23,865
You were going into the service.

319
00:10:24,045 --> 00:10:25,217
Or you were getting married.

320
00:10:25,397 --> 00:10:27,655
Living alone, just for the sake of

321
00:10:27,655 --> 00:10:28,718
it, was unusual,

322
00:10:28,718 --> 00:10:30,179
and for a young woman,

323
00:10:30,179 --> 00:10:32,636
many parents considered it out of the

324
00:10:32,636 --> 00:10:33,234
question.

325
00:10:33,584 --> 00:10:35,462
But if you did find a place,

326
00:10:35,462 --> 00:10:37,340
it might be a rented room in

327
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:38,480
a boarding house,

328
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:41,097
with a landlady who served breakfast at

329
00:10:41,097 --> 00:10:43,377
seven and locked the front door at

330
00:10:43,377 --> 00:10:43,847
eleven.

331
00:10:44,027 --> 00:10:46,660
Or a small apartment above a store,

332
00:10:46,660 --> 00:10:48,992
shared with a friend from work,

333
00:10:48,992 --> 00:10:50,271
with a hot plate,

334
00:10:50,271 --> 00:10:52,829
a sink, and a radiator that banged

335
00:10:52,829 --> 00:10:53,582
all night.

336
00:10:53,932 --> 00:10:56,282
There was no washing machine in most

337
00:10:56,282 --> 00:10:57,391
small apartments.

338
00:10:57,571 --> 00:11:00,205
On Saturday mornings you carried a bag

339
00:11:00,205 --> 00:11:02,770
of laundry to the new coin laundromat

340
00:11:02,770 --> 00:11:03,879
down the street,

341
00:11:03,879 --> 00:11:06,027
fed quarters into the machines,

342
00:11:06,027 --> 00:11:08,384
and sat on a plastic chair reading

343
00:11:08,384 --> 00:11:09,147
a magazine.

344
00:11:09,327 --> 00:11:10,755
It was one of the small,

345
00:11:10,755 --> 00:11:12,956
ordinary rituals of being on your own

346
00:11:12,956 --> 00:11:14,087
for the first time.

347
00:11:14,437 --> 00:11:17,386
Rent was cheap by today's standards.

348
00:11:17,566 --> 00:11:19,746
By the census of nineteen sixty,

349
00:11:19,746 --> 00:11:22,675
the typical American rent was about seventy

350
00:11:22,675 --> 00:11:23,765
dollars a month.

351
00:11:23,945 --> 00:11:26,755
Gas cost around thirty cents a gallon.

352
00:11:26,935 --> 00:11:29,149
A hamburger at a diner was a

353
00:11:29,149 --> 00:11:30,255
quarter or so.

354
00:11:30,435 --> 00:11:32,788
A pack of cigarettes was about the

355
00:11:32,788 --> 00:11:35,486
same, and almost everybody smoked them.

356
00:11:35,836 --> 00:11:38,959
And yes, almost everybody smoked.

357
00:11:39,139 --> 00:11:40,062
In the office,

358
00:11:40,062 --> 00:11:41,381
at the dinner table,

359
00:11:41,381 --> 00:11:43,426
in the car with the windows up,

360
00:11:43,426 --> 00:11:45,338
in the hospital waiting room.

361
00:11:45,518 --> 00:11:48,897
Doctors appeared in cigarette advertisements.

362
00:11:49,077 --> 00:11:50,989
It would be years before most people

363
00:11:50,989 --> 00:11:52,955
understood what it was doing to them.

364
00:11:53,305 --> 00:11:55,278
There were no credit cards in most

365
00:11:55,278 --> 00:11:56,265
people's wallets.

366
00:11:56,445 --> 00:11:57,935
If you wanted something,

367
00:11:57,935 --> 00:11:58,991
you saved for it,

368
00:11:58,991 --> 00:12:01,165
or you bought it on the installment

369
00:12:01,165 --> 00:12:02,903
plan, a few dollars a month,

370
00:12:02,903 --> 00:12:05,077
with a little payment book you took

371
00:12:05,077 --> 00:12:05,884
to the store.

372
00:12:06,064 --> 00:12:08,406
A charge account at the department store

373
00:12:08,406 --> 00:12:10,162
was a sign that you'd arrived.

374
00:12:10,513 --> 00:12:13,243
Your first big purchase might have been

375
00:12:13,243 --> 00:12:14,994
a radio, a record player,

376
00:12:14,994 --> 00:12:16,744
or a portable television,

377
00:12:16,744 --> 00:12:19,615
bought from the appliance store downtown.

378
00:12:19,795 --> 00:12:21,463
Maybe it was a good suit,

379
00:12:21,463 --> 00:12:24,466
for job interviews and weddings and funerals,

380
00:12:24,466 --> 00:12:26,668
the only one you owned for years.

381
00:12:26,848 --> 00:12:28,718
You took care of things then,

382
00:12:28,718 --> 00:12:31,039
because replacing them was not easy.

383
00:12:31,389 --> 00:12:33,775
You opened your first savings account at

384
00:12:33,775 --> 00:12:34,849
the bank downtown,

385
00:12:34,849 --> 00:12:36,996
and they gave you a little passbook.

386
00:12:37,176 --> 00:12:39,117
Every time you made a deposit,

387
00:12:39,117 --> 00:12:41,381
the teller stamped it and wrote the

388
00:12:41,381 --> 00:12:42,610
new balance in ink.

389
00:12:42,790 --> 00:12:44,474
Watching that number grow,

390
00:12:44,474 --> 00:12:46,028
a few dollars at a time,

391
00:12:46,028 --> 00:12:48,166
was one of the quiet pleasures of

392
00:12:48,166 --> 00:12:48,943
being grown.

393
00:12:49,293 --> 00:12:51,050
And at the end of the month,

394
00:12:51,050 --> 00:12:53,120
you sat down at the kitchen table

395
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:55,379
with a pencil and a little notebook,

396
00:12:55,379 --> 00:12:56,885
and you added it all up.

397
00:12:57,065 --> 00:12:57,414
Rent.

398
00:12:57,594 --> 00:12:58,650
Groceries.

399
00:12:58,830 --> 00:13:00,746
Bus fare or gas.

400
00:13:00,926 --> 00:13:03,469
Something for the church collection plate.

401
00:13:03,649 --> 00:13:05,174
Something for savings,

402
00:13:05,174 --> 00:13:06,075
if you could.

403
00:13:06,255 --> 00:13:08,455
Your parents had taught you that a

404
00:13:08,455 --> 00:13:10,008
dollar was hard to earn,

405
00:13:10,008 --> 00:13:11,108
and easy to lose.

406
00:13:11,458 --> 00:13:13,350
Now let's talk about love.

407
00:13:13,530 --> 00:13:16,042
Because if you turned eighteen in nineteen

408
00:13:16,042 --> 00:13:18,614
fifty-seven, there's a good chance you were

409
00:13:18,614 --> 00:13:20,229
married within a few years.

410
00:13:20,409 --> 00:13:22,336
In the late nineteen fifties,

411
00:13:22,336 --> 00:13:25,261
Americans married younger than at almost any

412
00:13:25,261 --> 00:13:27,654
other time in the twentieth century.

413
00:13:28,004 --> 00:13:30,348
According to the Census Bureau,

414
00:13:30,348 --> 00:13:33,071
the typical bride in those years was

415
00:13:33,071 --> 00:13:35,264
only around twenty years old,

416
00:13:35,264 --> 00:13:38,592
and the typical groom was around twenty-two.

417
00:13:38,772 --> 00:13:41,512
Today, both are about ten years older.

418
00:13:41,692 --> 00:13:43,917
Your parents might have married at that

419
00:13:43,917 --> 00:13:44,374
age too.

420
00:13:44,554 --> 00:13:46,539
It didn't seem young to anybody.

421
00:13:46,719 --> 00:13:48,170
It seemed like the next step.

422
00:13:48,521 --> 00:13:50,227
Dating had its rules.

423
00:13:50,407 --> 00:13:51,638
The boy asked.

424
00:13:51,818 --> 00:13:53,495
The boy picked her up at her

425
00:13:53,495 --> 00:13:55,352
front door, and met her father,

426
00:13:55,352 --> 00:13:57,449
who looked him over very carefully.

427
00:13:57,629 --> 00:13:58,627
The boy paid.

428
00:13:58,807 --> 00:14:00,714
A date might be a movie,

429
00:14:00,714 --> 00:14:02,621
a soda at the drugstore,

430
00:14:02,621 --> 00:14:04,687
a dance, or a drive out to

431
00:14:04,687 --> 00:14:05,402
the lake.

432
00:14:05,582 --> 00:14:07,562
And she was home by the time

433
00:14:07,562 --> 00:14:08,694
her father said,

434
00:14:08,694 --> 00:14:10,179
not one minute later.

435
00:14:10,530 --> 00:14:12,817
Going steady was serious business.

436
00:14:12,997 --> 00:14:14,904
A boy gave a girl his class

437
00:14:14,904 --> 00:14:16,741
ring, and she wore it on a

438
00:14:16,741 --> 00:14:18,296
chain around her neck,

439
00:14:18,296 --> 00:14:20,627
or wrapped it with tape or angora

440
00:14:20,627 --> 00:14:22,888
yarn so it would fit her finger.

441
00:14:23,068 --> 00:14:25,251
Or he gave her his letterman's jacket.

442
00:14:25,431 --> 00:14:27,416
Everyone at school knew what it meant.

443
00:14:27,767 --> 00:14:29,468
Double dates were common,

444
00:14:29,468 --> 00:14:31,034
two couples in one car,

445
00:14:31,034 --> 00:14:33,212
because only one of the boys had

446
00:14:33,212 --> 00:14:34,709
a car worth borrowing.

447
00:14:34,889 --> 00:14:36,584
You went to the drive-in,

448
00:14:36,584 --> 00:14:38,483
and the boys bought popcorn,

449
00:14:38,483 --> 00:14:40,856
and nobody watched very much of the

450
00:14:40,856 --> 00:14:41,263
movie.

451
00:14:41,443 --> 00:14:42,830
Or you went bowling,

452
00:14:42,830 --> 00:14:45,603
which was hugely popular in those years,

453
00:14:45,603 --> 00:14:48,098
with new alleys opening all over the

454
00:14:48,098 --> 00:14:48,653
country.

455
00:14:49,003 --> 00:14:50,677
And then, one evening,

456
00:14:50,677 --> 00:14:51,514
on a porch,

457
00:14:51,514 --> 00:14:52,960
or in a parked car,

458
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:55,319
or at the table in her mother's

459
00:14:55,319 --> 00:14:56,688
kitchen, he asked.

460
00:14:56,868 --> 00:14:59,283
The engagement ring might have cost him

461
00:14:59,283 --> 00:15:00,769
a couple of months' pay.

462
00:15:00,949 --> 00:15:03,283
The wedding was in the church you'd

463
00:15:03,283 --> 00:15:04,417
both grown up in,

464
00:15:04,417 --> 00:15:06,617
followed by cake and punch in the

465
00:15:06,617 --> 00:15:09,218
church hall, and a honeymoon at Niagara

466
00:15:09,218 --> 00:15:10,818
Falls if you were lucky,

467
00:15:10,818 --> 00:15:12,685
or a weekend at a lake cabin

468
00:15:12,685 --> 00:15:13,685
if you weren't.

469
00:15:14,036 --> 00:15:15,051
Before the wedding,

470
00:15:15,051 --> 00:15:16,441
there was a bridal shower,

471
00:15:16,441 --> 00:15:18,258
where the women of the family gave

472
00:15:18,258 --> 00:15:20,235
the bride everything a new home would

473
00:15:20,235 --> 00:15:20,502
need.

474
00:15:20,682 --> 00:15:21,565
A toaster.

475
00:15:21,745 --> 00:15:23,776
A set of mixing bowls.

476
00:15:23,956 --> 00:15:26,023
Towels with her new initials.

477
00:15:26,203 --> 00:15:28,423
Many couples received a set of china

478
00:15:28,423 --> 00:15:30,335
they would keep for the rest of

479
00:15:30,335 --> 00:15:32,432
their lives, and bring out only on

480
00:15:32,432 --> 00:15:34,098
Thanksgiving and Christmas.

481
00:15:34,448 --> 00:15:35,568
And very often,

482
00:15:35,568 --> 00:15:37,362
the babies came quickly.

483
00:15:37,542 --> 00:15:40,226
Nineteen fifty-seven was the peak of the

484
00:15:40,226 --> 00:15:40,897
baby boom.

485
00:15:41,077 --> 00:15:43,191
According to government records,

486
00:15:43,191 --> 00:15:45,965
about four point three million babies were

487
00:15:45,965 --> 00:15:47,682
born in America that year,

488
00:15:47,682 --> 00:15:49,796
more than in any year before it.

489
00:15:49,976 --> 00:15:51,308
If you married young,

490
00:15:51,308 --> 00:15:53,084
you were part of that story,

491
00:15:53,084 --> 00:15:55,177
whether you planned to be or not.

492
00:15:55,528 --> 00:15:56,837
When the baby came,

493
00:15:56,837 --> 00:15:58,905
the father did not go into the

494
00:15:58,905 --> 00:15:59,870
delivery room.

495
00:16:00,050 --> 00:16:02,229
He paced the waiting room down the

496
00:16:02,229 --> 00:16:04,344
hall, with other nervous fathers,

497
00:16:04,344 --> 00:16:06,331
smoking and checking his watch.

498
00:16:06,511 --> 00:16:08,949
When a nurse finally came out and

499
00:16:08,949 --> 00:16:10,574
told him it was a boy,

500
00:16:10,574 --> 00:16:11,313
or a girl,

501
00:16:11,313 --> 00:16:13,973
he handed out cigars to everybody in

502
00:16:13,973 --> 00:16:14,638
the room.

503
00:16:14,818 --> 00:16:16,513
Many of you were that father.

504
00:16:16,863 --> 00:16:18,458
For many young women,

505
00:16:18,458 --> 00:16:21,040
marriage also meant the end of the

506
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:21,344
job.

507
00:16:21,524 --> 00:16:24,471
Some companies expected a woman to leave

508
00:16:24,471 --> 00:16:25,723
when she married,

509
00:16:25,723 --> 00:16:28,375
and almost all expected her to leave

510
00:16:28,375 --> 00:16:30,069
when she was expecting.

511
00:16:30,249 --> 00:16:32,199
It wasn't always what she wanted.

512
00:16:32,379 --> 00:16:34,516
It was simply how things were done.

513
00:16:34,696 --> 00:16:36,636
Many of those women went back to

514
00:16:36,636 --> 00:16:37,667
work years later,

515
00:16:37,667 --> 00:16:39,849
and their daughters would grow up in

516
00:16:39,849 --> 00:16:41,244
a very different world.

517
00:16:41,594 --> 00:16:43,716
A young couple with a little money

518
00:16:43,716 --> 00:16:45,838
saved, and a veteran's loan if the

519
00:16:45,838 --> 00:16:47,024
husband had served,

520
00:16:47,024 --> 00:16:48,896
could buy a small house in one

521
00:16:48,896 --> 00:16:50,394
of the new subdivisions.

522
00:16:50,574 --> 00:16:52,624
Two bedrooms, a little yard,

523
00:16:52,624 --> 00:16:55,113
and a mortgage that felt enormous.

524
00:16:55,293 --> 00:16:57,434
Many young couples moved in with a

525
00:16:57,434 --> 00:16:59,826
card table, two chairs and a mattress,

526
00:16:59,826 --> 00:17:01,777
and filled the rest in over the

527
00:17:01,777 --> 00:17:02,155
years.

528
00:17:02,505 --> 00:17:04,818
Whether you were single or married,

529
00:17:04,818 --> 00:17:05,941
you needed a car.

530
00:17:06,121 --> 00:17:08,121
And in nineteen fifty-seven,

531
00:17:08,121 --> 00:17:11,334
American cars were at their most spectacular.

532
00:17:11,514 --> 00:17:13,338
Long, low, two-toned,

533
00:17:13,338 --> 00:17:14,902
covered in chrome,

534
00:17:14,902 --> 00:17:17,769
and growing fins on the back like

535
00:17:17,769 --> 00:17:18,898
rocket ships.

536
00:17:19,248 --> 00:17:20,750
The car of the year,

537
00:17:20,750 --> 00:17:22,928
for many people then and now,

538
00:17:22,928 --> 00:17:26,457
was the nineteen fifty-seven Chevrolet Bel Air.

539
00:17:26,637 --> 00:17:28,005
Turquoise and white,

540
00:17:28,005 --> 00:17:29,167
or red and white,

541
00:17:29,167 --> 00:17:31,697
with those sharp little fins and that

542
00:17:31,697 --> 00:17:32,791
gleaming grille.

543
00:17:32,971 --> 00:17:35,062
It cost a little over two thousand

544
00:17:35,062 --> 00:17:37,276
dollars new, which was far more than

545
00:17:37,276 --> 00:17:39,368
an eighteen-year-old could afford.

546
00:17:39,548 --> 00:17:40,593
But you could dream.

547
00:17:40,943 --> 00:17:43,040
Ford had the Fairlane and the sporty

548
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:44,147
little Thunderbird.

549
00:17:44,327 --> 00:17:46,919
Chrysler's cars had the biggest fins of

550
00:17:46,919 --> 00:17:49,512
all, and advertising that called it the

551
00:17:49,512 --> 00:17:50,376
forward look.

552
00:17:50,556 --> 00:17:53,233
And in September of nineteen fifty-seven,

553
00:17:53,233 --> 00:17:55,715
Ford introduced a whole new brand with

554
00:17:55,715 --> 00:17:56,825
enormous fanfare.

555
00:17:57,005 --> 00:17:58,828
It was called the Edsel.

556
00:17:59,178 --> 00:18:01,016
The Edsel was one of the most

557
00:18:01,016 --> 00:18:03,868
famous failures in American business history.

558
00:18:04,048 --> 00:18:06,978
The public found its vertical grille strange,

559
00:18:06,978 --> 00:18:08,541
and its price confusing,

560
00:18:08,541 --> 00:18:10,950
and the economy was turning down just

561
00:18:10,950 --> 00:18:11,862
as it arrived.

562
00:18:12,042 --> 00:18:13,515
Within a few years,

563
00:18:13,515 --> 00:18:14,445
it was gone.

564
00:18:14,625 --> 00:18:16,414
But if your uncle bought one,

565
00:18:16,414 --> 00:18:18,758
you probably still tease him about it.

566
00:18:19,108 --> 00:18:20,509
For many young men,

567
00:18:20,509 --> 00:18:22,869
the car was also their workshop.

568
00:18:23,049 --> 00:18:24,625
On Saturday afternoons,

569
00:18:24,625 --> 00:18:27,090
you'd find them in driveways and gas

570
00:18:27,090 --> 00:18:29,145
station bays all over America,

571
00:18:29,145 --> 00:18:31,337
with the hood up and their heads

572
00:18:31,337 --> 00:18:32,569
inside the engine.

573
00:18:32,749 --> 00:18:34,119
They traded parts,

574
00:18:34,119 --> 00:18:37,693
swapped advice, and argued endlessly about Ford

575
00:18:37,693 --> 00:18:38,682
versus Chevy.

576
00:18:38,862 --> 00:18:41,041
Some of them have never stopped arguing

577
00:18:41,041 --> 00:18:41,544
about it.

578
00:18:41,894 --> 00:18:44,587
Your own first car was probably used.

579
00:18:44,767 --> 00:18:46,532
A forty-nine Ford.

580
00:18:46,712 --> 00:18:50,323
A fifty-one Chevy with a cracked dashboard.

581
00:18:50,503 --> 00:18:53,014
You learned to change the oil yourself,

582
00:18:53,014 --> 00:18:54,624
to fix a flat on the side

583
00:18:54,624 --> 00:18:55,397
of the road,

584
00:18:55,397 --> 00:18:57,200
and to push it down the hill

585
00:18:57,200 --> 00:18:58,939
to get it started on a cold

586
00:18:58,939 --> 00:18:59,454
morning.

587
00:18:59,634 --> 00:19:01,097
And on Friday nights,

588
00:19:01,097 --> 00:19:02,072
you washed it,

589
00:19:02,072 --> 00:19:03,535
put on a clean shirt,

590
00:19:03,535 --> 00:19:05,137
and drove to her house.

591
00:19:05,487 --> 00:19:07,468
There were no seat belts in most

592
00:19:07,468 --> 00:19:09,635
cars, and nobody thought to ask for

593
00:19:09,635 --> 00:19:09,945
them.

594
00:19:10,125 --> 00:19:12,296
There was no air conditioning,

595
00:19:12,296 --> 00:19:14,829
either, so in summer you drove with

596
00:19:14,829 --> 00:19:17,362
all the windows down and your elbow

597
00:19:17,362 --> 00:19:18,809
resting on the door.

598
00:19:18,989 --> 00:19:21,227
And with the new highways being built,

599
00:19:21,227 --> 00:19:23,524
you started thinking about how far that

600
00:19:23,524 --> 00:19:24,643
car could take you.

601
00:19:24,823 --> 00:19:25,740
Chicago.

602
00:19:25,920 --> 00:19:26,849
California.

603
00:19:27,029 --> 00:19:27,795
Anywhere.

604
00:19:28,146 --> 00:19:30,237
Rock and roll was only a couple

605
00:19:30,237 --> 00:19:31,113
of years old,

606
00:19:31,113 --> 00:19:33,811
and it was still shocking the grown-ups.

607
00:19:33,991 --> 00:19:35,824
In nineteen fifty-seven,

608
00:19:35,824 --> 00:19:38,039
Elvis had All Shook Up at the

609
00:19:38,039 --> 00:19:39,413
top of the charts.

610
00:19:39,593 --> 00:19:41,509
Buddy Holly had That'll Be the Day.

611
00:19:41,689 --> 00:19:44,075
Jerry Lee Lewis was pounding the piano

612
00:19:44,075 --> 00:19:46,461
and setting the whole country on fire.

613
00:19:46,811 --> 00:19:48,456
And on August fifth,

614
00:19:48,456 --> 00:19:53,147
nineteen fifty-seven, a television show from Philadelphia

615
00:19:53,147 --> 00:19:54,299
went national.

616
00:19:54,479 --> 00:19:56,174
American Bandstand.

617
00:19:56,354 --> 00:19:58,142
Every weekday afternoon,

618
00:19:58,142 --> 00:20:01,121
a young host named Dick Clark introduced

619
00:20:01,121 --> 00:20:04,473
the records, and ordinary teenagers danced in

620
00:20:04,473 --> 00:20:06,037
front of the cameras.

621
00:20:06,217 --> 00:20:08,911
Kids across the country raced home to

622
00:20:08,911 --> 00:20:11,023
watch, and to copy the steps.

623
00:20:11,374 --> 00:20:12,655
On Saturday night,

624
00:20:12,655 --> 00:20:14,719
there was the drive-in movie,

625
00:20:14,719 --> 00:20:16,000
or the dance hall,

626
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:17,353
or the roller rink.

627
00:20:17,533 --> 00:20:19,882
You might see a monster movie,

628
00:20:19,882 --> 00:20:20,900
or a western,

629
00:20:20,900 --> 00:20:23,719
or a teenage drama that your parents

630
00:20:23,719 --> 00:20:24,894
disapproved of.

631
00:20:25,074 --> 00:20:27,643
And afterward, a booth at the diner,

632
00:20:27,643 --> 00:20:29,712
with a jukebox in the corner,

633
00:20:29,712 --> 00:20:31,996
and a plate of fries shared four

634
00:20:31,996 --> 00:20:32,353
ways.

635
00:20:32,703 --> 00:20:34,324
At the movies that year,

636
00:20:34,324 --> 00:20:36,487
Elvis starred in Jailhouse Rock,

637
00:20:36,487 --> 00:20:38,717
and girls screamed at the screen.

638
00:20:38,897 --> 00:20:40,075
And at Christmas,

639
00:20:40,075 --> 00:20:42,639
The Bridge on the River Kwai arrived,

640
00:20:42,639 --> 00:20:44,857
a war movie your father would go

641
00:20:44,857 --> 00:20:45,758
to see twice.

642
00:20:45,938 --> 00:20:48,893
Hollywood was fighting hard to pull people

643
00:20:48,893 --> 00:20:50,864
away from their televisions,

644
00:20:50,864 --> 00:20:52,130
with wide screens,

645
00:20:52,130 --> 00:20:54,030
bright color and big stars.

646
00:20:54,381 --> 00:20:55,573
That same year,

647
00:20:55,573 --> 00:20:58,990
a young writer named Jack Kerouac published

648
00:20:58,990 --> 00:21:01,851
a novel about driving back and forth

649
00:21:01,851 --> 00:21:04,632
across America, called On the Road.

650
00:21:04,812 --> 00:21:06,186
And on Broadway,

651
00:21:06,186 --> 00:21:09,192
a new musical about two rival gangs

652
00:21:09,192 --> 00:21:10,222
in New York,

653
00:21:10,222 --> 00:21:12,197
called West Side Story,

654
00:21:12,197 --> 00:21:13,914
opened in September.

655
00:21:14,094 --> 00:21:15,785
The culture was changing,

656
00:21:15,785 --> 00:21:17,272
and you could feel it,

657
00:21:17,272 --> 00:21:18,692
even in a small town.

658
00:21:19,042 --> 00:21:20,552
For many young people,

659
00:21:20,552 --> 00:21:22,956
the newest thing in their hands was

660
00:21:22,956 --> 00:21:24,672
a small transistor radio.

661
00:21:24,852 --> 00:21:25,955
For the first time,

662
00:21:25,955 --> 00:21:27,987
you could carry the music with you.

663
00:21:28,167 --> 00:21:29,001
To the beach,

664
00:21:29,001 --> 00:21:29,771
to the park,

665
00:21:29,771 --> 00:21:31,697
to your bedroom late at night.

666
00:21:31,877 --> 00:21:34,141
The radio was no longer the family's.

667
00:21:34,321 --> 00:21:35,296
It was yours.

668
00:21:35,646 --> 00:21:38,088
The boys wore their hair in crew

669
00:21:38,088 --> 00:21:40,378
cuts, or, if they were daring,

670
00:21:40,378 --> 00:21:43,355
swept back with grease into a ducktail.

671
00:21:43,535 --> 00:21:46,283
They wore white T-shirts and blue jeans

672
00:21:46,283 --> 00:21:47,621
rolled at the cuff.

673
00:21:47,801 --> 00:21:50,594
The girls wore saddle shoes and full

674
00:21:50,594 --> 00:21:52,844
skirts, pearls and cardigans,

675
00:21:52,844 --> 00:21:55,637
and their hair in ponytails or short

676
00:21:55,637 --> 00:21:56,102
curls.

677
00:21:56,282 --> 00:21:58,779
Your parents had opinions about all of

678
00:21:58,779 --> 00:21:58,976
it.

679
00:21:59,326 --> 00:22:02,233
If you were eighteen in nineteen fifty-seven,

680
00:22:02,233 --> 00:22:03,848
you remember that autumn,

681
00:22:03,848 --> 00:22:05,850
even if you don't remember why.

682
00:22:06,030 --> 00:22:07,703
Because in just a few weeks,

683
00:22:07,703 --> 00:22:10,450
three things happened that changed the country

684
00:22:10,450 --> 00:22:11,824
you were stepping into.

685
00:22:12,174 --> 00:22:14,491
In September, in Little Rock,

686
00:22:14,491 --> 00:22:18,087
Arkansas, nine Black teenagers tried to enter

687
00:22:18,087 --> 00:22:19,685
Central High School.

688
00:22:19,865 --> 00:22:21,228
They were your age,

689
00:22:21,228 --> 00:22:22,303
or close to it.

690
00:22:22,483 --> 00:22:24,178
Crowds screamed at them.

691
00:22:24,358 --> 00:22:26,768
The governor sent the National Guard to

692
00:22:26,768 --> 00:22:27,632
keep them out.

693
00:22:27,812 --> 00:22:30,472
And President Eisenhower sent soldiers of the

694
00:22:30,472 --> 00:22:33,014
hundred and first Airborne Division to walk

695
00:22:33,014 --> 00:22:34,314
them through the door.

696
00:22:34,664 --> 00:22:36,614
You watched it on the evening news.

697
00:22:36,794 --> 00:22:38,138
Kids your own age,

698
00:22:38,138 --> 00:22:40,155
carrying their schoolbooks,

699
00:22:40,155 --> 00:22:41,798
walking past bayonets.

700
00:22:41,978 --> 00:22:44,113
Whatever you thought about it then,

701
00:22:44,113 --> 00:22:46,065
you never forgot those pictures.

702
00:22:46,415 --> 00:22:48,358
Then, on October fourth,

703
00:22:48,358 --> 00:22:52,891
nineteen fifty-seven, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik,

704
00:22:52,891 --> 00:22:55,401
the first artificial satellite.

705
00:22:55,581 --> 00:22:58,684
It circled the Earth every ninety-six minutes,

706
00:22:58,684 --> 00:23:01,113
sending out a little beep that radio

707
00:23:01,113 --> 00:23:03,812
operators could hear all over the world.

708
00:23:03,992 --> 00:23:06,072
Some people stood in their yards at

709
00:23:06,072 --> 00:23:07,498
night trying to spot it.

710
00:23:07,849 --> 00:23:10,136
And here's a strange coincidence.

711
00:23:10,316 --> 00:23:11,885
That very same evening,

712
00:23:11,885 --> 00:23:14,546
a gentle new family comedy premiered on

713
00:23:14,546 --> 00:23:15,297
television.

714
00:23:15,477 --> 00:23:17,311
It was called Leave It to Beaver.

715
00:23:17,491 --> 00:23:18,999
So on the same night,

716
00:23:18,999 --> 00:23:22,159
Americans saw the most comforting picture of

717
00:23:22,159 --> 00:23:24,529
family life ever put on a screen,

718
00:23:24,529 --> 00:23:27,186
and heard that the Soviets had beaten

719
00:23:27,186 --> 00:23:28,335
them into space.

720
00:23:28,685 --> 00:23:30,061
For a young person,

721
00:23:30,061 --> 00:23:32,017
it was a confusing message.

722
00:23:32,197 --> 00:23:33,426
On one channel,

723
00:23:33,426 --> 00:23:36,376
a perfect family in a perfect house.

724
00:23:36,556 --> 00:23:39,454
On another, a news anchor talking about

725
00:23:39,454 --> 00:23:40,643
Soviet missiles.

726
00:23:40,823 --> 00:23:43,442
Many of you remember your parents' faces

727
00:23:43,442 --> 00:23:45,406
that week, quieter than usual,

728
00:23:45,406 --> 00:23:47,632
and the feeling that the world had

729
00:23:47,632 --> 00:23:50,250
suddenly become a little more dangerous.

730
00:23:50,601 --> 00:23:53,805
Overnight, everything changed in the schools.

731
00:23:53,985 --> 00:23:58,199
Science and mathematics became a national priority.

732
00:23:58,379 --> 00:23:59,497
Within a year,

733
00:23:59,497 --> 00:24:02,053
Congress passed a law to pay for

734
00:24:02,053 --> 00:24:04,927
science education and student loans.

735
00:24:05,107 --> 00:24:07,203
If you were thinking about college,

736
00:24:07,203 --> 00:24:09,838
suddenly the government wanted very much for

737
00:24:09,838 --> 00:24:10,436
you to go.

738
00:24:10,787 --> 00:24:12,876
America tried to answer quickly.

739
00:24:13,056 --> 00:24:14,521
On December sixth,

740
00:24:14,521 --> 00:24:18,833
nineteen fifty-seven, the Navy's Vanguard rocket rose

741
00:24:18,833 --> 00:24:21,680
about four feet off the launch pad,

742
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:24,283
and exploded on live television.

743
00:24:24,463 --> 00:24:25,949
The newspapers mocked it.

744
00:24:26,129 --> 00:24:28,254
Some called it Flopnik.

745
00:24:28,434 --> 00:24:30,849
But less than two months later,

746
00:24:30,849 --> 00:24:32,719
on January thirty-first,

747
00:24:32,719 --> 00:24:36,458
nineteen fifty-eight, America's first satellite,

748
00:24:36,458 --> 00:24:38,639
Explorer One, reached orbit.

749
00:24:38,989 --> 00:24:40,665
And that same autumn,

750
00:24:40,665 --> 00:24:43,459
a new strain of influenza swept the

751
00:24:43,459 --> 00:24:44,097
country.

752
00:24:44,277 --> 00:24:46,320
People called it the Asian flu.

753
00:24:46,500 --> 00:24:49,151
Schools emptied, offices ran short,

754
00:24:49,151 --> 00:24:51,195
and according to the C D C,

755
00:24:51,195 --> 00:24:54,905
about one hundred sixteen thousand Americans died

756
00:24:54,905 --> 00:24:56,949
from it over the next year.

757
00:24:57,129 --> 00:24:59,723
Many of you remember being sick in

758
00:24:59,723 --> 00:25:00,868
bed for a week,

759
00:25:00,868 --> 00:25:03,538
or a classmate who never came back.

760
00:25:03,888 --> 00:25:06,539
Then, as if all that wasn't enough,

761
00:25:06,539 --> 00:25:08,358
the economy slowed down.

762
00:25:08,538 --> 00:25:12,091
The recession of nineteen fifty-seven and fifty-eight

763
00:25:12,091 --> 00:25:13,901
hit the factory towns hard.

764
00:25:14,081 --> 00:25:17,398
According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics,

765
00:25:17,398 --> 00:25:20,940
unemployment climbed above seven percent by the

766
00:25:20,940 --> 00:25:23,276
summer of nineteen fifty-eight.

767
00:25:23,456 --> 00:25:26,116
Young workers, the last ones hired,

768
00:25:26,116 --> 00:25:28,623
were often the first ones let go.

769
00:25:28,973 --> 00:25:33,176
So adulthood, for an eighteen-year-old in nineteen

770
00:25:33,176 --> 00:25:35,950
fifty-seven, wasn't only freedom.

771
00:25:36,130 --> 00:25:37,841
It was responsibility,

772
00:25:37,841 --> 00:25:39,474
arriving all at once.

773
00:25:39,654 --> 00:25:41,558
A paycheck that had to stretch.

774
00:25:41,738 --> 00:25:44,048
A draft card in your wallet.

775
00:25:44,228 --> 00:25:45,341
A wedding ring,

776
00:25:45,341 --> 00:25:47,491
maybe, and a baby on the way.

777
00:25:47,671 --> 00:25:50,160
A world that seemed to change every

778
00:25:50,160 --> 00:25:52,152
time you turned on the news.

779
00:25:52,502 --> 00:25:54,551
A young husband who lost his job

780
00:25:54,551 --> 00:25:57,304
at the plant in nineteen fifty-eight didn't

781
00:25:57,304 --> 00:25:58,968
have much of a safety net.

782
00:25:59,148 --> 00:26:02,166
There was unemployment insurance for a while,

783
00:26:02,166 --> 00:26:03,441
and a few odd jobs,

784
00:26:03,441 --> 00:26:06,056
and family who helped where they could.

785
00:26:06,236 --> 00:26:08,561
Many young couples moved back in with

786
00:26:08,561 --> 00:26:10,509
their parents for a few months.

787
00:26:10,689 --> 00:26:12,308
Nobody talked about it much,

788
00:26:12,308 --> 00:26:13,870
but a lot of people did it.

789
00:26:14,220 --> 00:26:16,467
And yet, ask almost anyone who lived

790
00:26:16,467 --> 00:26:18,590
it, and they'll tell you there was

791
00:26:18,590 --> 00:26:20,338
something good about it too.

792
00:26:20,518 --> 00:26:21,888
You knew your neighbors.

793
00:26:22,068 --> 00:26:23,519
Your boss knew your name.

794
00:26:23,699 --> 00:26:25,876
A hard-working young person,

795
00:26:25,876 --> 00:26:27,975
with a high school diploma,

796
00:26:27,975 --> 00:26:29,219
could get a job,

797
00:26:29,219 --> 00:26:29,996
buy a car,

798
00:26:29,996 --> 00:26:31,162
start a family,

799
00:26:31,162 --> 00:26:34,350
and believe that tomorrow would be better

800
00:26:34,350 --> 00:26:35,205
than today.

801
00:26:35,385 --> 00:26:37,434
For a great many of you,

802
00:26:37,434 --> 00:26:38,032
it was.

803
00:26:38,382 --> 00:26:41,110
Adult life also meant belonging.

804
00:26:41,290 --> 00:26:42,962
You joined a church,

805
00:26:42,962 --> 00:26:44,384
a bowling league,

806
00:26:44,384 --> 00:26:45,554
a union local,

807
00:26:45,554 --> 00:26:47,310
the Elks, the Rotary,

808
00:26:47,310 --> 00:26:49,150
the women's auxiliary.

809
00:26:49,330 --> 00:26:52,319
On weeknights there were meetings and suppers

810
00:26:52,319 --> 00:26:53,382
and fundraisers.

811
00:26:53,562 --> 00:26:54,985
People knew who you were,

812
00:26:54,985 --> 00:26:56,978
and they noticed if you didn't show

813
00:26:56,978 --> 00:26:57,149
up.

814
00:26:57,329 --> 00:26:58,966
It could feel confining.

815
00:26:59,146 --> 00:27:01,143
It also meant you were never alone.

816
00:27:01,494 --> 00:27:03,932
You couldn't vote until you were twenty-one.

817
00:27:04,112 --> 00:27:05,415
In nineteen sixty,

818
00:27:05,415 --> 00:27:08,384
if you were born in nineteen thirty-nine,

819
00:27:08,384 --> 00:27:09,688
you finally could,

820
00:27:09,688 --> 00:27:13,164
and your first presidential election was Kennedy

821
00:27:13,164 --> 00:27:14,178
against Nixon.

822
00:27:14,358 --> 00:27:16,722
Many of you still remember walking into

823
00:27:16,722 --> 00:27:18,904
that voting booth for the very first

824
00:27:18,904 --> 00:27:21,511
time, and pulling the curtain closed behind

825
00:27:21,511 --> 00:27:21,753
you.

826
00:27:22,103 --> 00:27:25,104
If you turned eighteen in nineteen fifty-seven,

827
00:27:25,104 --> 00:27:27,211
you're in your late eighties now.

828
00:27:27,391 --> 00:27:29,189
You've seen the moon landing,

829
00:27:29,189 --> 00:27:30,677
the end of the Cold War,

830
00:27:30,677 --> 00:27:32,846
the arrival of the computer and the

831
00:27:32,846 --> 00:27:34,148
phone in your pocket.

832
00:27:34,328 --> 00:27:36,793
Your grandchildren have grandchildren,

833
00:27:36,793 --> 00:27:37,637
some of them.

834
00:27:37,817 --> 00:27:40,535
And still, if somebody plays All Shook

835
00:27:40,535 --> 00:27:42,252
Up, you know every word.

836
00:27:42,602 --> 00:27:44,866
You remember the smell of the plant

837
00:27:44,866 --> 00:27:47,131
floor, or the clatter of the typing

838
00:27:47,131 --> 00:27:47,455
pool.

839
00:27:47,635 --> 00:27:49,597
The weight of that first paycheck.

840
00:27:49,777 --> 00:27:52,099
The ring in its little velvet box.

841
00:27:52,279 --> 00:27:54,844
The sound of Sputnik's beep on the

842
00:27:54,844 --> 00:27:55,297
radio.

843
00:27:55,477 --> 00:27:58,070
The faces of the classmates you said

844
00:27:58,070 --> 00:28:00,519
goodbye to in June of fifty-seven,

845
00:28:00,519 --> 00:28:02,896
and the ones you never saw again.

846
00:28:03,246 --> 00:28:04,685
You grew up fast.

847
00:28:04,865 --> 00:28:07,176
Faster than young people do today.

848
00:28:07,356 --> 00:28:09,485
At eighteen you were working,

849
00:28:09,485 --> 00:28:11,173
or serving, or married,

850
00:28:11,173 --> 00:28:12,127
or all three.

